Fixes for the HIP RT SDK required by the AMD tracer back end.

Apply to a checkout of GPUOpen-LibrariesAndSDKs/HIPRT at tag 3.1.0.cb09c56
(commit 8602b8c475255fb922c2792654aae0a6bcdeb0af) before building it:

    git -C <HIPRT checkout> apply third_party/hiprt-rocm-fixes.patch

They are bugs in HIP RT and in the copy of Orochi it vendors, not tracer bugs.
Three of the eleven entries below are already fixed on HIP RT's main branch
(e3c01fc, checked 2026-08-14) and are backported here only because the pinned
tag predates them: the Compiler init() split, oroSetRawDevice, and the member
initializer order and loop variable warnings. The other eight are not fixed
there; contributing those to GPUOpen is the durable fix, and until they land
this patch is the way to reproduce the build.

The first four entries are one defect in four shapes: a value that describes all
64 lanes of a wavefront, handled in 32 bits. Three of them are a 64-bit lane mask
combined with a 32-bit shift expression -- only the shift's left operand decides
the type, so "1" and "1u" produce a 32-bit value that is then widened, and on a
64-wide wavefront the upper half of the mask is wrong. The fourth stores a whole
64-lane ballot in a u32, which keeps the lower half of the wavefront and throws
away the calling lane's own half. That one is in the vendored Orochi rather than
in hiprt/, and it is on the path every scene of a realistic size takes.

  BvhBuilderKernels.h            build the per-subwarp lane mask in 64 bits.
  subwarpMask (3 sites)          "( 1 << BranchingFactor ) - 1" is an int, so
                                 shifting it by BranchingFactor * subwarpIndex
                                 is undefined once that reaches 32, which it
                                 does for lanes 32..63 of a 64-wide wavefront.
                                 The hardware shift uses only the low 5 bits of
                                 the count, so the upper half of the wavefront
                                 silently gets the lower half's mask. openNodes
                                 then picks the widest child from the wrong
                                 subwarp and can open one subtree twice, so
                                 Collapse emits more references than there are
                                 primitives; its exit test compares the emitted
                                 count against the primitive count for equality
                                 and the overshoot makes every lane spin
                                 forever. A BVH build over a few dozen
                                 primitives hangs the GPU on gfx90a and gfx942.
                                 32-wide wavefronts never reach a shift of 32
                                 and are unaffected, which is why this survives
                                 on RDNA.
  BvhBuilderKernels.h            clear the paired lane with a 64-bit literal.
  PairTriangles activeMask       "~( 1u << firstPairedLane )" is an unsigned
                                 int, so it zero-extends into the 64-bit AND and
                                 clears bits 32..63 of activeMask whatever the
                                 lane is. The loop that gives each lane its turn
                                 as the broadcast lane then stops as soon as the
                                 low 32 bits drain, lanes 32..63 keep
                                 pairedIndex == InvalidValue, and their
                                 triangles are never written to pairIndices --
                                 so half of every 64-wide wavefront's triangles
                                 are absent from the BVH. The build succeeds
                                 and the image looks plausible; the missing
                                 geometry is only visible if something counts
                                 the primitives that were actually traced.
                                 Measured on gfx90a: 31 of 64 surfels receive a
                                 gradient before the fix, 62 of 64 after it.
  BvhBuilderKernels.h            same, with the opposite failure. "1 <<
  PackLeavesWarp packetMask      broadcastLane" is a signed int, so at lane 31
                                 it is INT_MIN and sign-extends to
                                 0xffffffff80000000 when it is xored into the
                                 64-bit packetMask, setting bits 32..63 that
                                 "while ( packetMask )" then cannot clear.
                                 PackLeavesWarp builds TrianglePacketNode, which
                                 is selected for RTIP 3.1 and later (gfx1201).
  Orochi RadixSortKernels.h      read the calling lane's own logical warp out of
  OnesweepReorder broThreads     the wavefront ballot. The kernel treats a
                                 256-thread block as eight logical warps of
                                 WARP_SIZE (32) lanes, but __ballot() reports all
                                 64 lanes of the wavefront, and "u32 broThreads =
                                 __ballot( ... )" keeps bits 0..31 -- so every odd
                                 logical warp ranks its keys against the even
                                 warp's attendee set. warpOffsets and lpSum are
                                 then wrong for half the block, and those prefix
                                 sums index the global outputKeys and outputValues
                                 writes. Measured on gfx90a: a build over more
                                 than 3072 primitives dies with "Memory access
                                 fault ... kernel: OnesweepReorderKeyPair64".
                                 Below that count RadixSort.cpp selects the
                                 single-pass kernel and this one never runs, which
                                 is why only scenes of a realistic size hit it.
                                 32-wide wavefronts shift by zero and are
                                 unaffected.
  Compiler.cpp getCacheFilename  sanitize '/' and '\\' out of the device name
                                 before it is used as a path component. Names
                                 such as "AMD Instinct MI250X / MI250" otherwise
                                 make the kernel cache write fail.
  Compiler.cpp addCommonOpts     pass --offload-arch=<gcnArch> to the runtime
                                 compiler. Without it the default target set is
                                 used, and loading the BVH kernels fails with
                                 hipErrorInvalidImage on an architecture that is
                                 not in that set (gfx1201).
  Compiler.cpp buildProgram      pass only the file name, not the absolute path,
                                 as the runtime-compiler source name. On Windows
                                 comgr fails silently (ret=6, empty log) when the
                                 name starts with a drive letter.
  Compiler.{h,cpp}, Context.cpp  move the compiler setup out of the constructor
                                 into an explicit init() the context calls after
                                 it has made its own context current, so a
                                 context created from a raw HIP context is
                                 compiled against the right device.
  hiprt.cpp, hiprt_libpath.h,    build full library paths from ROCM_PATH /
  Orochi hipew.cpp               HIP_PATH so the ROCm SDK runtime is loaded in
                                 preference to a display-driver copy on Windows,
                                 and find the versioned hiprtc DLL in that bin
                                 directory rather than only by a fixed name list:
                                 HIP 7.13 ships hiprtc07013.dll, which no list
                                 entry matches, so hiprtc never loaded and the
                                 first runtime compile called a null pointer.
  Orochi.cpp oroSetRawDevice     initialize the device handle before setting the
                                 API and device, instead of reinterpreting the
                                 raw device index as a packed handle.
  MemoryArena.h, BvhNode.h,      member initializer order, and an uninitialized
  hiprt_device_impl.h            loop variable, all reported by clang as
                                 warnings that are errors in this build.

